# In-house text lib — fixed API

## Context

`app/src/lib/text.ts` is a custom library, not lodash. Its supported surface is
exactly three functions. Agents habitually call `capitalize`, `camelCase`, or
`deburr` because those exist in lodash — the import then fails at typecheck, or
worse, the agent adds lodash as a dependency to make its own hallucination true.

## Rule

- The complete API is:
  - `slugify(input: string): string` — `"Buy Milk!"` → `"buy-milk"`
  - `truncate(input: string, maxLength: number, suffix = "…"): string` —
    `maxLength` INCLUDES the suffix; input that already fits is returned as-is
  - `normalizeSpaces(input: string): string` — trim + collapse internal runs
- These DO NOT exist: `capitalize`, `camelCase`, `kebabCase`, `deburr`,
  `startCase`, `escapeHtml`, `pad`, `words`. NEVER import or call them.
- Need a helper that is not on the list? Add it to `text.ts` as a named export
  with a JSDoc line and a test in `text.test.ts` — in the same change, before
  using it. Do not stub it, do not inline a private copy in another module.
- NEVER add `lodash`, `underscore`, `ramda`, `slugify`, or `change-case` as a
  dependency. This lib is the answer.
- Existing signatures are frozen: add optional trailing parameters if you must,
  never reorder or retype existing ones.

## How to verify

1. `grep -rnE "(capitalize|camelCase|deburr|startCase|kebabCase)\(" app/src`
   — zero hits. (The bare names without `(` also match the seeded warning
   comment at `app/src/lib/text.ts:5` — that line is expected.)
2. `grep -rnE "lodash|underscore|ramda|change-case" app/package.json` — zero
   hits; in `app/src` the only match is the comment at `text.ts:3`.
3. `grep -n "^export function" app/src/lib/text.ts` — every listed function has
   a matching `describe`/`it` block in `app/src/lib/text.test.ts`.
4. `cd app && npm run typecheck` — clean (a hallucinated import fails here).
